{
  "schema_version": "howl.exploration_result/v1",
  "exploration_id": "hd-20261008-143634-565a00716635",
  "parent_request_id": "cubs-r001-p2-critique",
  "objective": "Critique these six candidate software opportunities for the Chicago Cubs (an MLB club). For EACH candidate, attack: (1) plausibility of the baseball problem and whether the decision really recurs; (2) whether the decision-relevant data is lawfully public AND available at decision time (no hindsight); (3) actionability: what would a staff member do differently; (4) testability: what retrospective evaluation against a simple baseline is possible with public data; (5) existing alternatives (spreadsheets, public tools, vendor systems, what sophisticated clubs already have); (6) what would make this project LOOK useful while actually providing little or no baseball advantage. Then do a BLIND-SPOT analysis of the set as a whole: shared assumptions, user groups or decision types none of them cover, and failure modes common to all. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN lines for missing evidence, and IDEA lines only for a sharpened or replacement opportunity that the critique reveals. Prefix each line's content with the candidate's short name.",
  "originating_component": "operator",
  "authority": {
    "type": "ADVISORY",
    "executable": false
  },
  "candidates": [
    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261008-143634-565a00716635/candidates/0/0",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261008-143634-565a00716635",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r001-p2-critique",
      "objective": "Critique these six candidate software opportunities for the Chicago Cubs (an MLB club). For EACH candidate, attack: (1) plausibility of the baseball problem and whether the decision really recurs; (2) whether the decision-relevant data is lawfully public AND available at decision time (no hindsight); (3) actionability: what would a staff member do differently; (4) testability: what retrospective evaluation against a simple baseline is possible with public data; (5) existing alternatives (spreadsheets, public tools, vendor systems, what sophisticated clubs already have); (6) what would make this project LOOK useful while actually providing little or no baseball advantage. Then do a BLIND-SPOT analysis of the set as a whole: shared assumptions, user groups or decision types none of them cover, and failure modes common to all. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN lines for missing evidence, and IDEA lines only for a sharpened or replacement opportunity that the critique reveals. Prefix each line's content with the candidate's short name.",
      "text": "ASSUMPTION: callups: Recurrence is real but low-frequency. Promotion calls number in the dozens per season, and they are driven by roster slots, 40-man status, injuries and development plans more than by readiness estimates.\nASSUMPTION: callups: Public MiLB stats are available at decision time, but Statcast-style tracking coverage varies by level, so a translation model may rest on thin features such as outcomes and age relative to level.\nASSUMPTION: callups: A staff member would change little. They probably already read public translated stats such as FanGraphs and Baseball Savant, so uncertainty bands may only confirm a decision already made on roster grounds.\nASSUMPTION: callups: A retrospective test is feasible with public data. Use only information available as of each date, compare against a naive baseline (raw or league-adjusted rate stats with age), and score on later MLB outcomes. Survivorship bias is the main risk, because only promoted players have MLB outcomes.\nASSUMPTION: callups: Alternatives are existing public translation and projection systems and whatever sophisticated clubs hold internally. A public-data version is at best a baseline.\nASSUMPTION: callups: It would look useful through polished uncertainty bands that are never scored for calibration, in-sample fits, or accuracy gained mainly by predicting who is called up rather than who succeeds.\nASSUMPTION: rules_ledger: The decision recurs, but errors are probably rare. A well-kept spreadsheet plus a rules-literate staffer may already deliver most of the value.\nASSUMPTION: rules_ledger: Public transaction logs may not capture option-year edge cases, injured-list time or non-public clauses, so the ledger could be wrong in the cases where it matters most.\nASSUMPTION: rules_ledger: The action is concrete, a warning before a move becomes impossible. But the value is avoided error, not information, so it is only worth building if past errors happened.\nASSUMPTION: rules_ledger: Retrospective testing is weak. Public logs rarely show near-misses, so one could only check whether the ledger reproduces known statuses.\nASSUMPTION: rules_ledger: It would look useful through an impressive dashboard of dates that duplicates what the transactions office already tracks, with no measured reduction in errors.\nASSUMPTION: rules_ledger: Rule and CBA changes would need manual upkeep, and a stale encoding is worse than a person who knows the rules.\nASSUMPTION: return_to_play: Public IL data carries only coarse injury descriptions, which may not be specific enough to define meaningful comparables.\nASSUMPTION: return_to_play: Clinical judgment and private medical information dominate timelines, so a public distribution may anchor staff on a misleading base rate.\nASSUMPTION: return_to_play: The action is limited to roster contingency planning, such as how long to plan for a replacement. This is plausibly useful but small.\nASSUMPTION: return_to_play: A test is possible. Compare predicted days missed from public comparables against a simple baseline such as the league median by coarse injury category. The IL placement date is known at decision time, but injury labels may be revised afterward.\nASSUMPTION: return_to_play: It would look useful through wide, honest-looking distributions that no one acts on, or through a tool that implicitly pulls in private Cubs medical data and creates a privacy and compliance problem.\nASSUMPTION: return_to_play: Clubs with internal medical databases likely already do this better, so a public version is parity at best.\nASSUMPTION: pitch_predictability: The decision is real and recurs daily. Public pitch data (count, prior pitch, handedness) is lawful and available, and an opponent could build the same model.\nASSUMPTION: pitch_predictability: Predictability is not the same as exploitability. A predictable pitch may still be effective if its quality is high, and reducing predictability may cost pitch quality.\nASSUMPTION: pitch_predictability: Actionability is plausible, since a pitching coach or catcher could change sequencing. Real clubs probably already run versions of this, so the gain is likely parity.\nASSUMPTION: pitch_predictability: Testing is feasible on public data. Compare a count-only baseline with richer models and check whether predictability measured early in a season relates to later outcomes. The link to run prevention is unproven.\nASSUMPTION: pitch_predictability: It would look useful through entropy numbers or predictive accuracy that mostly reflect each pitcher's pitch mix, which is already visible in public data.\nASSUMPTION: normalization: The decision recurs, and it overlaps heavily with callups. Both need the same league-season adjustments and could be one project.\nASSUMPTION: normalization: Public MiLB data and documented rule changes are available by season. But rule regimes change mid-season and sample sizes per league-season are small, so adjustment factors may be noisy.\nASSUMPTION: normalization: The staff action is a different promotion order or prospect ranking. Public sites and park factors partly normalize already, so the marginal change may be small.\nASSUMPTION: normalization: A retrospective test is possible. Check whether adjusted stats predict next-level or MLB performance better than raw stats with level and age. Rule regimes confound this, because few players cross regimes.\nASSUMPTION: normalization: It would look useful through elaborate park and rule adjustments that fit past data well but add nothing to out-of-sample prediction over a simple level-and-age baseline.\nASSUMPTION: opp_bullpen: Reliever usage is public after the fact through box scores, but availability at decision time depends on unobservable warm-ups, injuries and internal usage rules, so forecasts may be only slightly better than a naive rule.\nASSUMPTION: opp_bullpen: Actionability is limited. Lineups are usually set before the opponent's bullpen use is known, and the Cubs' moves are mostly dictated by game state.\nASSUMPTION: opp_bullpen: Testing is feasible. Predict each opponent's relievers used on each date and compare against a naive baseline such as no pitches in the last two days. A test with no real baseline would only show that rested pitchers pitch.\nASSUMPTION: opp_bullpen: Advance staff already estimate this informally, and sophisticated clubs probably have fuller internal usage models.\nASSUMPTION: opp_bullpen: It would look useful through high accuracy on easy cases, such as relievers who pitched yesterday, while saying nothing about the closer calls where the decision actually changes.\nUNKNOWN: callups: public_milb_statcast_coverage_by_level\nUNKNOWN: callups: cubs_existing_translation_models\nUNKNOWN: rules_ledger: cubs_roster_admin_error_history\nUNKNOWN: rules_ledger: completeness_of_public_option_status_data\nUNKNOWN: return_to_play: granularity_of_public_injury_descriptions\nUNKNOWN: return_to_play: cubs_current_timeline_estimation_process\nUNKNOWN: pitch_predictability: evidence_linking_sequence_entropy_to_run_prevention\nUNKNOWN: pitch_predictability: cubs_existing_sequence_audit\nUNKNOWN: normalization: minor_league_rule_regime_effect_sizes\nUNKNOWN: normalization: public_milb_park_factor_quality\nUNKNOWN: opp_bullpen: forecast_accuracy_vs_naive_rule\nUNKNOWN: opp_bullpen: unobserved_bullpen_warmups_effect\nCONFLICT: pooled_assumption_attribution (the assumption lists are pooled across many ideas and not mapped to the six candidates, so some listed assumptions, such as tipping or grounds crew, do not belong to these six)\nASSUMPTION: blind_spots: All six lean on public data and assume a public-data tool can beat what sophisticated clubs already hold. At best they reach parity.\nASSUMPTION: blind_spots: None addresses amateur draft or international scouting, hitter or pitcher development decisions, in-season roster optimization, or players themselves as users.\nASSUMPTION: blind_spots: Four of the six (callups, normalization, return_to_play, rules_ledger) are front-office or analyst tools. Coaches and game-day staff are barely covered, and those groups' willingness to use a tool is untested.\nASSUMPTION: blind_spots: Common failure modes include small samples, no pre-registered baseline, hindsight leakage from later-revised data, adoption failure, and no measurement of changed decisions.\nASSUMPTION: blind_spots: Success is judged by prediction accuracy rather than by changed decisions and downstream wins, so a tool could be accurate and still add nothing.\nIDEA: shared: a pre-registered backtest harness on public data, with naive baselines and time-stamped inputs, that scores any candidate on out-of-sample performance before anything is built\nIDEA: callups: merge callups and normalization into one translation baseline, evaluated on out-of-sample MLB outcomes against a level-and-age baseline, before any interface is built\nIDEA: rules_ledger: first audit whether any rules-related errors have occurred, via interviews with staff, and build the ledger only if they have",
      "condition": "dream",
      "trust": "UNVERIFIED",
      "status": "UNRESOLVED",
