    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261008-143634-565a00716635/candidates/0/1",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261008-143634-565a00716635",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r001-p2-critique",
      "objective": "Critique these six candidate software opportunities for the Chicago Cubs (an MLB club). For EACH candidate, attack: (1) plausibility of the baseball problem and whether the decision really recurs; (2) whether the decision-relevant data is lawfully public AND available at decision time (no hindsight); (3) actionability: what would a staff member do differently; (4) testability: what retrospective evaluation against a simple baseline is possible with public data; (5) existing alternatives (spreadsheets, public tools, vendor systems, what sophisticated clubs already have); (6) what would make this project LOOK useful while actually providing little or no baseball advantage. Then do a BLIND-SPOT analysis of the set as a whole: shared assumptions, user groups or decision types none of them cover, and failure modes common to all. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN lines for missing evidence, and IDEA lines only for a sharpened or replacement opportunity that the critique reveals. Prefix each line's content with the candidate's short name.",
      "text": "ASSUMPTION: CallUpCalibration (1 recurrence) Promotion decisions recur, but they are driven by 40-man space, option status, injuries and service-time, not only readiness. A stats-to-MLB translation may inform a small share of actual decisions.\nASSUMPTION: CallUpCalibration (2 data) Public MiLB stats are lawful. Statcast-style tracking coverage differs by level and year, and the final-season stat line is hindsight-contaminated unless snapshots are frozen as of each decision date.\nASSUMPTION: CallUpCalibration (3 action) Uncertainty bands only matter if staff would change timing or choice. If promotions are mostly driven by roster need, the output is decoration.\nASSUMPTION: CallUpCalibration (4 test) Backtest on public history: forecast the first-MLB-season outcome from snapshots at promotion date and compare against a baseline of raw age-adjusted OPS/ERA with regression to the mean. Check interval coverage and calibration. Promotion selection bias means only promoted players have MLB outcomes.\nASSUMPTION: CallUpCalibration (5 alternatives) Public projection systems and translation methods exist, and sophisticated clubs likely hold better private models using scouting and tracking data. A public-data version is a baseline at best.\nASSUMPTION: CallUpCalibration (6 looks-useful) Polished bands with no demonstrated coverage, or a tool tuned in-sample to past call-ups, would look rigorous while adding nothing over public projections.\nUNKNOWN: CallUpCalibration public_milb_statcast_coverage_by_level\nUNKNOWN: CallUpCalibration how_often_cubs_promotion_timing_is_readiness_driven_vs_roster_driven\nASSUMPTION: RosterLedger (1 recurrence) The problem is real and recurs through the season, but errors may be rare because staff already track options. Value depends on a history of missed or costly moves, which is not shown.\nASSUMPTION: RosterLedger (2 data) Public transactions are lawful and timely. Option years, optional-assignment days, and some service-time and IL edge cases are not fully public, so the ledger needs internal inputs the project cannot test publicly.\nASSUMPTION: RosterLedger (3 action) A warning before a move becomes impossible changes behavior only if the deadlines were being missed. Otherwise it only reminds.\nASSUMPTION: RosterLedger (4 test) Public data can reconstruct past option and eligibility states and count how often clubs lost a player to Rule 5 or burned an option inefficiently. That requires a baseline of a rules-literate spreadsheet, and the frequency of such errors may be too low to measure.\nASSUMPTION: RosterLedger (5 alternatives) Front offices almost certainly have a spreadsheet or internal database for this, and league systems handle official status. The gain is reliability only.\nASSUMPTION: RosterLedger (6 looks-useful) A dashboard that restates already-known deadlines looks useful, and rule-encoding bugs after CBA changes could produce false confidence.\nUNKNOWN: RosterLedger cubs_roster_admin_error_history\nUNKNOWN: RosterLedger completeness_of_public_option_status_data\nASSUMPTION: ReturnToPlay (1 recurrence) IL events recur, but a timeline decision is made by medical staff on individual diagnosis. A comparables search may not change that.\nASSUMPTION: ReturnToPlay (2 data) Public IL entries have coarse injury labels and no imaging, grade or surgery detail. Public comparables then mix heterogeneous injuries. Any use of Cubs medical data creates privacy and CBA issues and falls outside the public-data constraint.\nASSUMPTION: ReturnToPlay (3 action) The plausible use is roster contingency planning, such as how long to hold a replacement. Even there, a wide distribution may be uninformative.\nASSUMPTION: ReturnToPlay (4 test) Backtest predicted days missed from public IL history against a baseline of the median by injury label or the league-wide median. Compare error on out-of-time seasons. Reporting inconsistencies and retroactive IL placement degrade labels.\nASSUMPTION: ReturnToPlay (5 alternatives) Public injury trackers exist, and clubs with internal medical records and team physicians already hold better information. This is near-certain parity at best.\nASSUMPTION: ReturnToPlay (6 looks-useful) Tight-looking distributions from vague labels, or post-return performance comparisons confounded by survivorship and by who gets reinstated, would seem informative.\nUNKNOWN: ReturnToPlay granularity_of_public_injury_descriptions\nUNKNOWN: ReturnToPlay cubs_medical_staff_current_use_of_comparables\nASSUMPTION: PitchSeqSelfScout (1 recurrence) Pregame planning recurs every series and the question of whether pitchers are predictable is real. It is plausible but no evidence shows predictability is exploitable by hitters.\nASSUMPTION: PitchSeqSelfScout (2 data) Public pitch type, count and handedness data is lawful and available before the next game, so there is no hindsight problem. Hitters use richer cues than this model captures.\nASSUMPTION: PitchSeqSelfScout (3 action) Staff would vary sequencing. Reducing predictability may cost pitch quality, so the gain is not guaranteed, and catchers and pitchers already adjust by feel.\nASSUMPTION: PitchSeqSelfScout (4 test) Check whether lower-entropy pitchers allow worse outcomes than predicted by pitch quality, using league-wide public data and a baseline of simple pitch-mix-by-count averages. Predictability of pitch choice and run prevention must be separated from pitch quality and selection by game state.\nASSUMPTION: PitchSeqSelfScout (5 alternatives) Sophisticated clubs already run sequencing audits, and public tools show pitch mix by count. The edge is parity.\nASSUMPTION: PitchSeqSelfScout (6 looks-useful) An entropy score that is high or low without being tied to run value looks scientific. Predictable from count alone is also the normal, near-optimal behavior.\nUNKNOWN: PitchSeqSelfScout evidence_linking_sequence_entropy_to_run_prevention\nUNKNOWN: PitchSeqSelfScout cubs_existing_sequence_audit\nASSUMPTION: CrossLeagueNorm (1 recurrence) It recurs for every minor league evaluation, but it overlaps CallUpCalibration. It is really a preprocessing step, not a separate product.\nASSUMPTION: CrossLeagueNorm (2 data) Rule regime and park factors are lawful and public. Effect sizes of rule experiments are estimated from a few league-seasons, confounded with who plays there, and rules change faster than data accumulates.\nASSUMPTION: CrossLeagueNorm (3 action) Evaluators would discount or boost stats by league. They may already do this mentally, so the change is unclear.\nASSUMPTION: CrossLeagueNorm (4 test) Test whether adjusted stats predict next-level or MLB performance better than raw age-adjusted stats and published park factors. Natural experiments occur when a rule changes within a league, but sample and roster churn limit power.\nASSUMPTION: CrossLeagueNorm (5 alternatives) Public sites partly normalize already, and clubs hold private adjustments. A public-data version risks only matching them.\nASSUMPTION: CrossLeagueNorm (6 looks-useful) An adjustment table with many decimals looks precise while its error exceeds its correction.\nUNKNOWN: CrossLeagueNorm minor_league_rule_regime_effect_sizes\nUNKNOWN: CrossLeagueNorm public_milb_park_factor_quality\nCONFLICT: CallUpCalibration_vs_CrossLeagueNorm overlapping scope and shared data dependency, which suggests merging them and testing once\nASSUMPTION: OppBullpenForecast (1 recurrence) Late-game choices against likely relievers recur nightly. But the lineup, pinch-hit and platoon decisions are largely set by matchup and roster, and knowing who is unavailable is a marginal input.\nASSUMPTION: OppBullpenForecast (2 data) Public box scores give appearances and pitch counts after the game, so a forecast for tonight is legitimate. Injuries, internal usage limits and warm-up-only workload are not public, so true availability is only partly observable.\nASSUMPTION: OppBullpenForecast (3 action) Staff might hold a pinch-hitter or adjust a platoon start. The effect of any such change is small, and bullpens can be reshaped by the opponent in-game.\nASSUMPTION: OppBullpenForecast (4 test) Backtest availability predictions from rolling workload against the naive rule of used yesterday or at 25+ pitches means unavailable, and score precision, recall and Brier loss. Then ask whether predictions would have changed any decision. That second step cannot be done with public data alone.\nASSUMPTION: OppBullpenForecast (5 alternatives) Advance scouts and analysts already estimate this, and public workload trackers exist. Clubs likely have more complete information, including real warm-up information.\nASSUMPTION: OppBullpenForecast (6 looks-useful) High accuracy on trivial cases such as pitched three days in a row looks good, but it matches the naive rule and does not affect any decision.\nUNKNOWN: OppBullpenForecast forecast_accuracy_vs_naive_rule\nUNKNOWN: OppBullpenForecast unobserved_bullpen_warmups_effect\nASSUMPTION: BlindSpots Shared assumption: public data suffices to create an edge. For most of these candidates sophisticated clubs hold richer private data, so the likely outcome is parity.\nASSUMPTION: BlindSpots Shared assumption: staff behavior would change when handed a tool. Adoption, trust and incentives are untested for all six, and none states a measurable decision change.\nASSUMPTION: BlindSpots Common failure mode: evaluating on outcomes without freezing the information available at decision time, which creates hindsight leakage. Also small samples, selection effects, and comparing against weak baselines instead of simple ones.\nASSUMPTION: BlindSpots The candidate set is analyst-centric and information-centric. It does not cover amateur draft and scouting workflow, pro acquisition and trade valuation, hitter development, strength and conditioning, ticketing and business operations, or defensive and baserunning in-game decisions.\nASSUMPTION: BlindSpots The supplied context mixes assumptions from different ideas under each candidate, and no source records support any claim, so every statement above is unverified critique.\nUNKNOWN: BlindSpots cubs_internal_analytics_capabilities\nUNKNOWN: BlindSpots supplied_evidence_records_for_any_candidate\nIDEA: DecisionSnapshotBacktestHarness Build a frozen as-of-date public-data archive and a scoring harness that tests any of these six predictions against simple baselines. Users are analysts, the decision is which tool deserves staff time, and the intervention is an evaluation framework rather than a product.\nASSUMPTION: DecisionSnapshotBacktestHarness Public historical snapshots, such as dated transaction logs and box scores, can be reconstructed without hindsight leakage. This needs verification.",
      "condition": "dream",
      "trust": "UNVERIFIED",
      "status": "UNRESOLVED",
      "authority": {
        "type": "ADVISORY",
        "executable": false
      },
