      "objective": "Critique these six candidate software opportunities for the Chicago Cubs (an MLB club). For EACH candidate, attack: (1) plausibility of the baseball problem and whether the decision really recurs; (2) whether the decision-relevant data is lawfully public AND available at decision time (no hindsight); (3) actionability: what would a staff member do differently; (4) testability: what retrospective evaluation against a simple baseline is possible with public data; (5) existing alternatives (spreadsheets, public tools, vendor systems, what sophisticated clubs already have); (6) what would make this project LOOK useful while actually providing little or no baseball advantage. Then do a BLIND-SPOT analysis of the set as a whole: shared assumptions, user groups or decision types none of them cover, and failure modes common to all. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN lines for missing evidence, and IDEA lines only for a sharpened or replacement opportunity that the critique reveals. Prefix each line's content with the candidate's short name.",
      "text": "ASSUMPTION: Callup-calibration: the promotion decision recurs, but it is driven by 40-man status, roster need, service-time and option rules, and development-staff judgment, so a translation estimate may rarely be the binding input.\nASSUMPTION: Callup-calibration: public MiLB stats exist at decision time, but public MiLB tracking coverage varies by level and season (UNVERIFIED); the best inputs (scouting, private tracking) are not public, so the tool is a baseline, not an edge.\nASSUMPTION: Callup-calibration: a staffer would change promotion timing or the comparison set only if bands move a decision across a threshold; bands wide enough to be honest may never do so.\nASSUMPTION: Callup-calibration: a retrospective test is feasible: for each past league-season, use only data through the promotion date, predict MLB outcomes after promotion, and compare against a simple baseline (raw wRC+ or ERA with a fixed level discount). Check calibration of the intervals, not just point error. Selection bias applies because only promoted players have MLB outcomes.\nASSUMPTION: Callup-calibration: public alternatives already exist (FanGraphs, Baseball Prospectus, and Baseball Savant minor-league pages and translation or projection systems), and sophisticated clubs likely have better models. The software overlaps heavily with these.\nASSUMPTION: Callup-calibration: it would look useful with polished dashboards, narrow bands from an overconfident model, or a backtest scored on players who reached MLB anyway or tuned with later-season data (hindsight leakage).\nASSUMPTION: Roster-ledger: option years, optional assignments, DFA timing and Rule 5 deadlines recur every season, and mistakes are costly, but they are likely rare in a club with dedicated baseball operations staff.\nASSUMPTION: Roster-ledger: public transactions can reconstruct part of the status, but option-year counts, minimum-days-down tracking, service time and injured-list edge cases may not be fully public or may rest on rules that change with each CBA. Treat completeness as UNKNOWN.\nASSUMPTION: Roster-ledger: the actionable output is an alert before a move becomes costly. Value depends on the club's current error history, which is not supplied. A spreadsheet plus a rules-literate staffer may already suffice.\nASSUMPTION: Roster-ledger: retrospective testing is weak. Public data cannot show a missed move that was never made, so you could only replay known historical cases and count how many the ledger would have flagged. That is a unit test, not evidence of advantage.\nASSUMPTION: Roster-ledger: it would look useful with a good-looking calendar of deadlines for facts staff already know, or by being tested only on cases chosen in hindsight.\nASSUMPTION: RTP-comparables: IL placements recur often, but timelines come mainly from private diagnosis, imaging and clinician judgment; public IL records give only a coarse injury label (granularity UNKNOWN), so comparables may mislead.\nASSUMPTION: RTP-comparables: public IL data are lawfully available, but the diagnosis at decision time is private. Putting private Cubs medical data into the tool would need a separate privacy and governance review. Public return dates are labelled after the fact.\nASSUMPTION: RTP-comparables: the actionable change is a contingency plan, such as whether to call up a replacement or stash an IL slot. A distribution wide enough to be honest may not change that choice.\nASSUMPTION: RTP-comparables: the backtest would predict days missed from information known at IL placement and compare against a baseline of the league-wide median by IL type. Selection and reporting bias is a risk because teams describe injuries inconsistently and sometimes vaguely.\nASSUMPTION: RTP-comparables: medical departments and vendors probably already hold richer internal databases, and public injury trackers exist. It would look useful with fine-grained comparable \"matches\" that are really noise from small samples.\nASSUMPTION: Pitch-predictability: the pregame game-plan decision recurs every series, but predictability from count, prior pitch and batter handedness is a coarse model, and the real game plan is driven by hitter-specific weaknesses and pitch quality.\nASSUMPTION: Pitch-predictability: public pitch-by-pitch data is lawful and available before the game, so there is no hindsight problem if only past games are used. But opponents' actual models and the catcher's in-game adaptation are not visible.\nASSUMPTION: Pitch-predictability: the staff action would be to change pitch mix or sequencing. This may trade lower predictability for worse pitch quality, so lower predictability need not mean fewer runs.\nASSUMPTION: Pitch-predictability: a falsifiable test is whether sequence entropy or model predictive accuracy on earlier games predicts run prevention in later games after controlling for pitch quality, against a baseline of pitch quality alone. Evidence linking predictability to results is UNKNOWN.\nASSUMPTION: Pitch-predictability: sophisticated clubs likely already run this self-scout. It would look useful with accurate next-pitch prediction scores that merely reflect obvious count effects, or with pitcher charts that nobody can act on.\nASSUMPTION: Cross-league-normalization: promotion comparison across affiliates recurs constantly, and rule regimes (ABS, pitch clock, park) differ, so the problem is plausible. The effect sizes of rule regimes are UNKNOWN.\nASSUMPTION: Cross-league-normalization: public stats and park information are available at decision time, but rule changes and few league-seasons give little data for estimating adjustments. Rosters turn over, and the adjustments partly confound player quality.\nASSUMPTION: Cross-league-normalization: the action is a fairer comparison, but it matters mainly when it flips a ranking, which may be uncommon. Public sites already partly normalize by league and park.\nASSUMPTION: Cross-league-normalization: the backtest compares against raw league-relative stats or existing public park-adjusted ones, scored on out-of-sample MLB outcomes. Compare adjusted versus unadjusted rank correlation using only information through the evaluation date.\nASSUMPTION: Cross-league-normalization: it would look useful with many adjustment factors fit to a handful of league-seasons, which overfits and looks rigorous.\nASSUMPTION: Opp-bullpen: lineup and pinch-hit decisions recur daily, but the manager and coaches already estimate who is likely to pitch, and knowing it must change the choice for the forecast to matter.\nASSUMPTION: Opp-bullpen: public box scores are lawful and available the morning of the game. They omit warmups, injuries, undisclosed rest, and the opposing club's private usage rules, so accuracy may be capped.\nASSUMPTION: Opp-bullpen: the staff would reorder a lineup, hold a pinch hitter, or choose a platoon starter. The effect on win probability is probably small because lineup order matters little, and unavoidable constraints bind the choice.\nASSUMPTION: Opp-bullpen: the test is easy and falsifiable. Predict each day's available relievers from prior-day usage and compare against a naive rule such as \"pitched yesterday and threw more than a fixed pitch count means unavailable.\" Use only prior data. Whether the gain converts to runs is UNKNOWN.\nASSUMPTION: Opp-bullpen: public availability-style trackers and clubs' advance scouts already do this. It would look useful with a high forecast hit rate that mostly reflects the naive rule.\nASSUMPTION: Shared-blind-spot: all six lean on public data, so the best case is parity with clubs holding private tracking, medical and scouting data; none shows a mechanism for a durable edge.\nASSUMPTION: Shared-blind-spot: none establishes the Cubs' current process, staff size, or error history, and these are UNKNOWN. A tool built without that is guessing about the need.\nASSUMPTION: Shared-blind-spot: adoption is unaddressed. Staff must trust, see and use outputs at the moment of decision. No candidate names a workflow or an owner.\nASSUMPTION: Shared-blind-spot: backtests are proxies for the metric, not for decisions. None defines the counterfactual, the cost of a wrong alert, or a win-value estimate. Small samples, multiple comparisons and retrospective tuning threaten all of them.\nASSUMPTION: Shared-blind-spot: the set mostly covers analysts, front office and coaches. It barely covers amateur and international scouting, strength and conditioning, mental performance, and clubhouse or equipment operations. Draft and trade valuation, contract or free-agent decisions, and minor-league logistics beyond promotion are largely absent.\nASSUMPTION: Shared-blind-spot: shared failure modes include dashboards that report accuracy without decisions changed, leakage of post-decision information, privacy and rules compliance concerns, and tools that duplicate public sites.\nUNKNOWN: cubs_current_decision_processes_and_tooling\nUNKNOWN: cubs_roster_admin_error_history\nUNKNOWN: public_milb_tracking_coverage_by_level\nUNKNOWN: granularity_of_public_injury_descriptions\nUNKNOWN: evidence_linking_sequence_predictability_to_run_prevention\nUNKNOWN: minor_league_rule_regime_effect_sizes\nUNKNOWN: win_value_of_bullpen_forecast_driven_lineup_changes\nIDEA: Decision-change audit (replacement): for a small set of recurring decisions, log pre-decision recommendations and what staff actually did, then score the outcome with a pre-registered baseline. First measure how often an output would have changed a choice before building any model.\nIDEA: Sharpened roster-ledger test: have a baseball-operations lawyer or analyst list past near-misses from public transaction reports (known only after the fact) as a regression check. Then measure only alert timeliness and false-alarm rate, not claimed wins.",
      "condition": "dream",
      "trust": "UNVERIFIED",
      "status": "UNRESOLVED",
      "authority": {
        "type": "ADVISORY",
        "executable": false
      },
      "claims": [
