    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261008-143634-565a00716635/candidates/0/3",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261008-143634-565a00716635",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r001-p2-critique",
      "objective": "Critique these six candidate software opportunities for the Chicago Cubs (an MLB club). For EACH candidate, attack: (1) plausibility of the baseball problem and whether the decision really recurs; (2) whether the decision-relevant data is lawfully public AND available at decision time (no hindsight); (3) actionability: what would a staff member do differently; (4) testability: what retrospective evaluation against a simple baseline is possible with public data; (5) existing alternatives (spreadsheets, public tools, vendor systems, what sophisticated clubs already have); (6) what would make this project LOOK useful while actually providing little or no baseball advantage. Then do a BLIND-SPOT analysis of the set as a whole: shared assumptions, user groups or decision types none of them cover, and failure modes common to all. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN lines for missing evidence, and IDEA lines only for a sharpened or replacement opportunity that the critique reveals. Prefix each line's content with the candidate's short name.",
      "text": "ASSUMPTION: META - No evidence records or fact keys were supplied, so there are no FACT or CITE lines. Every critique below is speculative and unverified.\nASSUMPTION: META - The candidate assumption lists are pooled across unrelated ideas and are not mapped to their candidates. Several (tipping, grounds crew, replay) belong to ideas outside these six, so they cannot be treated as support for any one of them.\n\nASSUMPTION: CALLUP_TRANSLATOR - Problem plausibility: promotion decisions recur, but they are driven by roster slots, 40-man status, injuries and development goals as much as by projected performance. A translated-stats forecast may address only a small part of the decision.\nASSUMPTION: CALLUP_TRANSLATOR - Data: public MiLB box-score stats are lawful and timely. Tracking data coverage differs by level and is unverified, and scouting-grade inputs are private and out of scope.\nASSUMPTION: CALLUP_TRANSLATOR - Actionability: staff would change a call-up only if the uncertainty band is wide enough to alter a ranking. Otherwise it is a display of what they already believe.\nASSUMPTION: CALLUP_TRANSLATOR - Testability: backtest translated MiLB lines against later MLB outcomes for players who were actually promoted, using year-by-year training cutoffs, against a naive baseline such as a raw-stat or age-adjusted rule. Survivorship bias is a threat, because only promoted players generate MLB samples. Interval calibration (coverage rates) is a stronger test than point error.\nASSUMPTION: CALLUP_TRANSLATOR - Alternatives: public projection systems and translation methods already exist, and sophisticated clubs likely hold better internal models. A public-data version is at best a baseline.\nASSUMPTION: CALLUP_TRANSLATOR - Looks useful but isn't: polished uncertainty bands on small samples, backtests that leak league-season context from later years, and accuracy measured against a weak baseline rather than the public systems.\nUNKNOWN: CALLUP_TRANSLATOR - public_milb_statcast_coverage_by_level, and how often the Cubs' promotions hinge on projection rather than roster mechanics.\n\nASSUMPTION: ROSTER_LEDGER - Problem plausibility: the rules are real and the deadlines are hard. But errors may be rare, and a rules-literate operations staffer with a spreadsheet may already prevent them.\nASSUMPTION: ROSTER_LEDGER - Data: transactions are public, but option-year counts, service-time edge cases and injured-list interactions may not be fully reconstructible. The ledger would depend on internal records anyway.\nASSUMPTION: ROSTER_LEDGER - Actionability: the warning must arrive early enough to change a move, and someone must own acting on it.\nASSUMPTION: ROSTER_LEDGER - Testability: public data supports little. You could replay past public transactions for violations or near-misses, but internal error history is private. A baseline of a manual calendar would be hard to beat visibly.\nASSUMPTION: ROSTER_LEDGER - Alternatives: league transaction systems, spreadsheets and likely existing club tooling.\nASSUMPTION: ROSTER_LEDGER - Looks useful but isn't: a dashboard with many alerts and no avoided losses, or deadline reminders for items staff already track.\nUNKNOWN: ROSTER_LEDGER - cubs_roster_admin_error_history, completeness_of_public_option_status_data.\n\nASSUMPTION: RTP_COMPS - Problem plausibility: injury timelines recur, but medical staff set them from private exams and imaging. Public IL labels are coarse.\nASSUMPTION: RTP_COMPS - Data: public IL entries are lawful but carry limited detail, and injury descriptions may be vague, delayed or inconsistent. The IL date is a roster decision, not an injury date, so it can mislead.\nASSUMPTION: RTP_COMPS - Actionability: the output would shift roster contingency planning only if the comparable distribution is tighter than staff priors. Wide distributions mostly confirm uncertainty.\nASSUMPTION: RTP_COMPS - Testability: forecast days missed by injury type using only information public at the placement date. Compare against a baseline of the league-wide median for IL type, or a position-only baseline, and score interval coverage. Confounding by team medical practice and roster need is a risk.\nASSUMPTION: RTP_COMPS - Alternatives: public injury trackers exist, and clubs with internal medical databases likely already do this better.\nASSUMPTION: RTP_COMPS - Looks useful but isn't: backtests that use diagnoses revealed after the placement date (hindsight), or attractive comparables that are not predictive.\nASSUMPTION: RTP_COMPS - Risk: shared tooling must not take in private Cubs medical data.\nUNKNOWN: RTP_COMPS - granularity_of_public_injury_descriptions.\n\nASSUMPTION: SEQ_SELFSCOUT - Problem plausibility: pregame planning recurs. But predictability from count, previous pitch and handedness is only a weak proxy for exploitability, which also depends on pitch quality and location.\nASSUMPTION: SEQ_SELFSCOUT - Data: public pitch-by-pitch data is lawful and available after each game. Pregame use depends on prior games only, so a strict walk-forward test is needed.\nASSUMPTION: SEQ_SELFSCOUT - Actionability: a coach changes sequencing only if reduced predictability improves outcomes. Making a pitcher less predictable can cost pitch quality.\nASSUMPTION: SEQ_SELFSCOUT - Testability: compare sequence entropy or predictability with run prevention in later games. Use a baseline of league-average predictability, and check whether outcomes differ for high- versus low-predictability pitchers. Small samples per pitcher will dominate.\nASSUMPTION: SEQ_SELFSCOUT - Alternatives: public pitch-mix tools and likely internal audits at sophisticated clubs. The edge may be parity.\nASSUMPTION: SEQ_SELFSCOUT - Looks useful but isn't: an entropy chart with no link to runs, or a flagged pattern that is really a pitch-quality or situational artifact.\nUNKNOWN: SEQ_SELFSCOUT - evidence_linking_sequence_entropy_to_run_prevention, cubs_existing_sequence_audit.\n\nASSUMPTION: MILB_CONTEXT_NORM - Problem plausibility: league and park differences are real and the comparison recurs. But public sites partly adjust already, and overlap with CALLUP_TRANSLATOR is large, so it is arguably one project.\nASSUMPTION: MILB_CONTEXT_NORM - Data: public stats and rule-change dates are lawful and usable. Rule-regime effects are confounded with talent and call-up churn, and single league-seasons give few samples.\nASSUMPTION: MILB_CONTEXT_NORM - Actionability: staff would alter promotion timing or prospect ranking only if the adjustment moves players across decision thresholds.\nASSUMPTION: MILB_CONTEXT_NORM - Testability: test whether adjusted stats predict later performance better than raw stats, using players who move between leagues or rule regimes as natural checks. Baselines are raw stats and simple park factors.\nASSUMPTION: MILB_CONTEXT_NORM - Alternatives: public park factors, league-level adjusted metrics, and likely internal models.\nASSUMPTION: MILB_CONTEXT_NORM - Looks useful but isn't: over-fit regime coefficients from tiny samples, or adjustments that change values trivially next to the noise.\nUNKNOWN: MILB_CONTEXT_NORM - minor_league_rule_regime_effect_sizes, public_milb_park_factor_quality.\n\nASSUMPTION: OPP_BULLPEN - Problem plausibility: reliever usage affects lineup and pinch-hit choices daily. But opposing managers' choices also depend on private warm-up, health and front-office information.\nASSUMPTION: OPP_BULLPEN - Data: public box scores and pitch counts are lawful and available before game time, but they omit unrecorded warm-ups and injuries, which the Cubs would not see either.\nASSUMPTION: OPP_BULLPEN - Actionability: a lineup or pinch-hit change must follow from a forecast. Often a manager already assumes the opponent's best relievers are used when needed, so the forecast may change little.\nASSUMPTION: OPP_BULLPEN - Testability: strong. Predict which relievers appear using only the prior-day data and compare against naive rules such as \"rested if not used yesterday\". Score by log loss or Brier score. Then test whether any decisions would have differed.\nASSUMPTION: OPP_BULLPEN - Alternatives: advance scouts, public usage trackers and likely club tools.\nASSUMPTION: OPP_BULLPEN - Looks useful but isn't: high forecast accuracy that merely reproduces the naive rule, or accuracy on easy cases only.\nUNKNOWN: OPP_BULLPEN - forecast_accuracy_vs_naive_rule, unobserved_bullpen_warmups_effect.\n\nASSUMPTION: BLINDSPOTS - Shared assumption: public data plus a model equals an edge. Several candidates are parity with sophisticated clubs at best, and the set never shows a Cubs-specific bottleneck.\nASSUMPTION: BLINDSPOTS - Shared assumption: adoption by busy staff. None of the candidates describes how it would reach a decision-maker, or who is accountable when the tool is wrong.\nASSUMPTION: BLINDSPOTS - Shared failure modes: hindsight leakage in backtests, small samples, weak or missing baselines, survivorship bias, and measuring forecast accuracy rather than changed decisions or wins.\nASSUMPTION: BLINDSPOTS - Uncovered user groups and decisions: amateur draft and scouting, in-season pitching staff workload, defense and positioning, ticket and fan-facing operations, and business-side staff. Most candidates also focus on information tools rather than on coordination, data-entry burden or error prevention.\nIDEA: BLINDSPOTS - Merge CALLUP_TRANSLATOR and MILB_CONTEXT_NORM into one baseline: a public-data translation model with walk-forward calibration, scored against raw-stat and public-projection baselines. Only if it beats those baselines would any added staff tooling be justified.\nIDEA: OPP_BULLPEN - Start with a pre-registered forecast-only test, run as a spreadsheet, and record which Cubs decisions would have changed. Build software only if the forecast beats the naive rule and decisions change.\nUNKNOWN: BLINDSPOTS - cubs_internal_analytics_capabilities, existing_cubs_decision_review_process, and the rate at which each candidate decision recurs per season.",
