        "objective": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs?\n\nDo not assume the product. Explore different opportunity classes: different users inside a baseball organization (front office, scouting, player development, coaching, medical/performance staff, analysts, game-day staff), different recurring decisions, and different mechanisms of value (better information, faster combination of signals, reduced costly uncertainty, process reliability, avoided errors). Include boring-but-useful interventions and non-obvious possibilities. Also name places where software is unnecessary because a spreadsheet, an existing public tool, or a manual process already suffices, and places where sophisticated clubs likely already have the advantage.\n\nOUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA line names one distinct baseball problem, the intended user, the concrete repeated decision, and the software intervention. Follow each IDEA with ASSUMPTION lines for what must be true (including whether lawfully accessible public data could support it) and UNKNOWN lines for missing evidence. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information (scouting, medical, biometric, contract, or proprietary tracking data).",
        "text": "IDEA: Self-scouting of pitch-sequence predictability for the pitching coach and catchers; the repeated decision is pregame game-plan design; software would measure how predictable the club's own pitchers' next-pitch choices are from count, previous pitch, and batter handedness, which is the same model an opponent could build.",
        "condition": "dream",
        "trust": "UNVERIFIED",
        "status": "UNRESOLVED",
        "authority": {
          "type": "ADVISORY",
          "executable": false
        },
